WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Flight 13's ship 40 splashdown and v3 satellite checkout move the first orbital tower catch one flight closer. Ship 40 completed a precise ocean splashdown after delivering stable reentry data through the plasma phase. Aviation Week imagery shows visible tile patterns on the recovered upper stage. The flight cleared a ship catch attempt for flight 14, the first planned orbital return to the tower. The program now shifts focus to booster catch hardware validation.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:58.980
The first location on the next mission. Remember we covered the ongoing flight test campaign yesterday. Today's results move the catch goal forward by clearing the ship recovery for the next attempt. The same mission delivered the first on orbit proof of the next starlink generation. The initial v3 satellites deployed their solar arrays and fired their thrusters successfully. They established both radio frequency and laser inter satellite links with the existing constellation.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:29.140
On board cameras returned imagery of the starship upper stage during the flight. These steps marked the first public validation of the v3 bus design. The starlink account on x posted the update directly. A Falcon 9 lifted off overnight from Cape Canaveral carrying the classified NROL 95 payload. The booster performed a down range landing on a drone ship and the launch produced an audible sonic boom across central flow.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:47.540
A second Falcon 9 is scheduled to lift off from Vandenberg Space Force Base on Friday evening. Both missions add flight experience to the active booster fleet under the current manifest. Local spotters are already tracking the planned down range recovery zone for the Vandenberg flight.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:55.420
Recent analysis shows that skilled production of the current tiled heat shield system can still reach acceptable per flight costs.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:08.180
The study compares material and refurbishment expenses against projected flight rates. It concludes that reuse margins remain viable if tile replacement stays below a defined threshold per mission.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:23.440
The report directly counters earlier assessments that the tiled approach would prove uneconomic at high cadence. Super micro and Dell are supplying modular rack systems that SpaceX is integrating into new AI focused facilities.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:36.060
Each unit arrives pre-integrated with power distribution and liquid cooling manifolds. The building block approach allows incremental power and cooling additions without full facility redesigns.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:47.880
An independent forecast now aligns with earlier projections that SpaceX linked AI infrastructure will reach 8 gigawatts of capacity by 2027 to 2028.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:58.360
Cursor announced a partner program that pairs its editor with reference architectures from AWS, BCG, Databricks, McKinsey, NVIDIA and Snowflake.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:09.580
The effort aims to produce documented deployments that other organizations can replicate. The program focuses on measurable adoption metrics and repeatable integration patterns.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:19.120
Observers on X noted that the V3 satellites returned the first public images of a starship upper stage taken from another spacecraft in orbit.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:26.100
Reddit threads highlighted the exact timing of the laser link acquisition sequence shown in the star link update.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:33.740
Aviation week readers pointed out the visible tile patterns on ship 40 in post-flight imagery released by the company.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:43.460
Worth keeping an eye on, a US House Democrat has asked Elon Musk to address emissions and water use tied to XAI data center construction.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:53.180
The inquiry focuses on power generation choices and cooling demands at the plant sites. SpaceX has not yet responded to the request.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:04:10.940
The letter highlights the scale of the facilities now under active procurement. On the AI front, Cursor's new benchmark partners program brings AWS, BCG, Databricks, McKinsey, NVIDIA and Snowflake together to publish reference enterprise deployments.

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:18.340
Super micro and Dell are shipping modular rack systems that SpaceX is installing in its expanding AI facilities.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:30.740
An independent report now projects the same eight gigawatts of SpaceX linked AI data center capacity by 2027 to 2028 that earlier forecasts had outlined.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:39.780
The partner program is intended to create replicable stacks that combine cursors editor with enterprise grade infrastructure and governance tools.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:52.300
From an engineering standpoint, the modular data center blocks now arriving from super micro and Dell change the economics of scaling AI infrastructure by collapsing the traditional build wants model.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:05:07.440
Instead of pouring a single large haul whose power and cooling capacity must be sized for the final load years in advance, each block ships with its own power distribution, liquid cooling manifolds and network spine already integrated.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:16.860
The raw material cost of the steel racks copper bus work and aluminum cold plates inside one block is only a few percent of the delivered price.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:27.300
The multiplier comes from the certification integration testing and rapid deployment logistics that let SpaceX at hundreds of megawatts without halting existing clusters.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:42.180
Because the blocks are electrically and thermally self contained incremental capacity can be brought online in weeks rather than months directly shortening the time between capital outlay and revenue generating inference or training runs.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:50.980
This approach also reduces the gap between the cost of the underlying silicon metal and kilowatt hours and the cost of a fully commissioned rack.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:06:00.900
The design choice trades a modest increase in per block overhead for the ability to match compute growth to actual demand rather than long range forecasts.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:10.300
The blocks function like shipping containers for power and cooling allowing the operator to treat each addition as an independent verifiable unit.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:20.420
The result is a lower effective cost per delivered GPU hour once utilization begins because idle over provisioned infrastructure is minimized.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:30.020
The next measurable milestone will be how quickly the first eight gigawatts tronch moves from rack delivery to sustained multi exaflop utilization.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:40.620
Early data from the initial installations will show whether the interconnect and power handoff between blocks maintains the low latency performance required for large training runs.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:52.940
If the blocks achieve the projected deployment cadence, the same modular logic could later extend to orbital compute nodes where mass and power constraints make traditional hall style construction impossible.

